  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q58-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "58",
    "prompt": "When the monic cubic polynomial $P(x)$ is divided by $x^2-3x+2$, the remainder is $x-1$. The graph of $y=P(x)$ has a $y$-intercept of $-1$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p25-q58-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $x=1$ is a zero.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(1)=0$, so $x=1$ is a zero.",
          "worked": "Since $P(x)$ is monic and cubic, its quotient is a monic linear polynomial, which we can write as $x-a$. The division identity gives\n$$P(x)=(x^2-3x+2)(x-a)+(x-1).$$\nSubstituting $x=1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}P(1)&=(1-3+2)(1-a)+(1-1)\\\\P(1)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $x=1$ is a zero.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q58-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the multiplicity of the zero at $x=1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p25-q58-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Multiplicity 3.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p25-q58-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use the y-intercept in the division identity to obtain ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "P(x)=(x-1)^3",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Using the division identity from part (a), the $y$-intercept gives $P(0)=-1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}P(0)&=(0^2-3(0)+2)(0-a)+(0-1)\\\\P(0)&=-2a-1.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $-1=-2a-1$, so $a=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(x)&=x(x^2-3x+2)+(x-1)\\\\P(x)&=x^3-3x^2+3x-1\\\\P(x)&=(x-1)^3.\\end{align*}$$\nThe factor $x-1$ occurs three times, so the zero at $x=1$ has multiplicity $3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      }
    ]
  },
